From 6477be4eeb37712d69827308f6ede1d1d74c5ce4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: snarayana <snarayana@student.ethz.ch>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:36:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(verify): a heavy-tailed max_z needs the pooled tail,
 not 16 run maxima

The first threshold for max_z was a log-normal prediction bound over the
K calibration runs' maxima. On an MXFP4 model it raised 4 false alarms
in 60 held-out benign runs (nominal alpha = 0.01): one confident token
(p = 0.988) moves 10 sigma under a per-request kernel setting the 16
calibration runs never sampled. Run maxima are heavy-tailed (fitted GPD
shape 0.14-0.47 on BF16, FP8 and MXFP4), and 16 of them cannot pin that.

max_z's threshold now comes from all K x N calibration tokens: a
generalized Pareto tail fitted (probability-weighted moments) to the
largest 5% of their leave-one-out |z|, extrapolated to per-token
probability alpha / (3 N) -- a union bound over the capture's tokens,
valid however unevenly the noise falls on them -- and never below the
largest calibration maximum.

rms_z and shift now cap each token's |z| at the calibration's 99.9th
percentile: the extremes are max_z's job, and one fragile token could
otherwise fire an average over a few hundred tokens.

On the CPU study (Qwen3-0.6B BF16, FP8, MXFP4; 147 injected faults):
false alarms 3/180 at --calibrate 16 and 2/132 at 32, i.e. ~1.6%
against a nominal 1% (fixed tolerances 1e-6, 0.05, 0.1: 180/180), with
every fault whose KL exceeds 3x the benign noise still caught (63/63).
The recommendation becomes --calibrate 32.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
